---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Authority is recognised" currentPath="/manifesto/foundations/authority">

  <article class="principle-document">
    <header class="principle-header">
      <div class="principle-meta">
        <span>I.2</span>
        <span>E01-P02</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Authority is recognised</h1>
      <p class="abstract">Authority emerges through coherence held over time, not assertion or display.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="principle-content">
      <p class="foundation-line">Authority is not asserted.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">It is recognised.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p class="foundation-line">It does not arrive through confidence alone.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">It arrives through consistency.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>Authority is felt when something holds — when it does not bend at the first sign of resistance.</p>

          <blockquote>Authority begins the moment a decision is made and held.</blockquote>

          <p class="foundation-line">Not defended.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">Not repeatedly justified.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">Held.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p class="foundation-line">Without decisions, there is no authorship — only negotiation.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">And negotiation, when constant, dissolves clarity.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>Many people mistake authority for certainty. In reality, authority is commitment.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">It is deciding, then allowing the work to exist long enough to be understood.</p>

          <blockquote>Authority does not speak the loudest. It speaks last — or not at all.</blockquote>

          <p class="foundation-line">Volume is often used to cover uncertainty.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p class="foundation-line">The more something is explained, the more fragile it becomes.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">Confident work does not rush to clarify itself.</p>

          <p>It allows misunderstanding to pass, trusting that alignment will come from those who are meant to stay.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p class="foundation-line">This is uncomfortable at first.</p>

          <p>Silence can feel like absence when you are used to feedback. But authority grows in that silence.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <blockquote>Authority is tested at the edges.</blockquote>

          <p class="foundation-line">In moments of pushback.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">In requests framed as collaboration.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">In suggestions that feel small, reasonable, harmless.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">This is where authority either holds — or dissolves.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p class="foundation-line">Holding the line does not require confrontation.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">It requires calm repetition.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">This is intentional.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">This is the decision.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">This is where we stop.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">When the line is held gently, trust often follows.</p>

          <p>Not because others agree — but because clarity feels stable.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <blockquote>Authority is often mistaken for control.</blockquote>

          <p class="foundation-line">Control seeks obedience.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">Authority seeks alignment.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p class="foundation-line">Control manages behaviour.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">Authority sets conditions.</p>

          <p>When you are clear about what belongs, there is less need to manage how others respond.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">The work becomes the reference point, not your explanation of it.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <blockquote>Authority is responsibility.</blockquote>

          <p class="foundation-line">Responsibility to decide what enters the work and what does not.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">That responsibility cannot be delegated.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>Authority is rarely noticed in the moment. It is felt afterward — when decisions hold, when the work continues to function without supervision.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">Its absence, however, is immediate.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">Authority is missing when everything feels up for debate.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">When nothing can land.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">When the work keeps asking to be reassured.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <blockquote>Authority creates stillness.</blockquote>

          <p>There is a moment when holding a decision feels exposed. When explanation would feel easier than stillness.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">This is the moment authority begins.</p>

          <p class="foundation-line">Not when others agree.</p>
          <p class="foundation-line">But when you remain steady anyway.</p>

          <div class="foundation-pause" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <blockquote>Authority is not confidence without doubt. It is steadiness in the presence of it.</blockquote>

          <aside class="foundation-reflection">
            <h2>Reflection</h2>
            <p>Where are you revisiting decisions that have already been made?</p>
            <p>Where would holding the line create clarity rather than conflict?</p>
            <p>What decision are you ready to stand behind quietly?</p>
          </aside>

      <h2>Citation</h2>
      <p class="citation-text">This principle may be cited as: <strong>I.2 (Authority)</strong> or by plate reference <strong>E01-P02</strong>.</p>
    </section>
  </article>

</BaseLayout>

